{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In drug-likeness optimization tasks (task_id=103), edits that retain the Murcko scaffold (murcko_scaffold_retained=true) induce smaller absolute changes in synthetic accessibility (|SA_delta|) than edits that break the scaffold.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the magnitude of synthetic accessibility changes between scaffold-retaining and scaffold-breaking successful edits for task 103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id==103 and non-null SA_delta.\n2. Partition rows into two groups by murcko_scaffold_retained (true vs false).\n3. Compute |SA_delta| for each row.\n4. Summarize group means and variances of |SA_delta|.\n5. Perform a two-sample t-test (or Welch’s t-test if variances differ) comparing |SA_delta| between the two groups.\n6. As a non-parametric check, perform a Mann–Whitney U test.\n7. Report effect size (Cohen’s d) and confidence intervals.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of group means, standard deviations, and counts of |SA_delta|.\n2. t-test results (t-statistic, df, p-value, confidence interval).\n3. Mann–Whitney U test result (U statistic, p-value).\n4. Effect size estimate."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The complexity of an edit, measured by the length of the edit description string, negatively impacts the probability of success across all tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify how edit description length influences success probability when controlling for task and action type.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet (successes) and train_neg.parquet (failures). Add success flag (1/0).\n2. Drop rows with null edit.\n3. Compute edit_length = number of characters in the edit string for each row.\n4. Fit a logistic regression: success ~ edit_length + C(task_id) + C(action_type).\n5. Examine the coefficient for edit_length (sign, SE, p-value).\n6. Plot predicted success probability vs. edit_length at reference levels of task_id and action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Logistic regression summary (coefficients, SE, p-values).\n2. Plot of predicted success probability vs. edit_length.\n3. Interpretation of the edit_length coefficient."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For permeability improvement tasks (task_id=105), insert operations achieve a higher median target_delta than replace or delete operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare median permeability gains across action types for task 105 in successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id==105 and non-null target_delta.\n2. Group by action_type and compute medians and interquartile ranges of target_delta.\n3. Perform a Kruskal-Wallis test to assess differences in distributions across the three action types.\n4. If significant, conduct post-hoc pairwise Mann–Whitney U tests with Bonferroni correction.\n5. Visualize boxplots of target_delta by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of median and IQR of target_delta by action_type.\n2. Kruskal–Wallis test result (H statistic, p-value).\n3. Post-hoc pairwise test results.\n4. Boxplot visualization."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful edits exhibit greater variability in molecular weight change (MW_delta) than successful edits, even after accounting for task differences.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether variance of MW_delta differs between successes and failures across tasks.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets; add success flag.\n2. Drop rows with null MW_delta.\n3. For each task_id, compute sample variance of MW_delta separately for success and failure groups.\n4. Perform Levene’s test for equality of variances within each task_id.\n5. Summarize across tasks: count of tasks where variance differs significantly.\n6. Optionally, fit a linear model of |MW_delta| with success and task interaction to confirm.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of variances by task and success flag.\n2. Levene’s test results for each task (W-statistic, p-value).\n3. Summary count of tasks with significant variance differences."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Edits involving ring-opening or ring-closing modifications (edits containing numeric ring labels) have a lower success rate than non-ring edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess how ring edits affect overall success probability.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets; add success flag.\n2. Define ring_edit = true if the edit string contains any digit (0–9), else false.\n3. Tabulate counts of success/failure by ring_edit flag.\n4. Perform a chi-square test of independence between ring_edit and success.\n5. Fit logistic regression: success ~ ring_edit + C(task_id) + C(action_type) to estimate adjusted odds ratio.\n6. Report odds ratio and confidence interval for ring_edit.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table of ring_edit vs. success.\n2. Chi-square test result (χ², df, p-value).\n3. Logistic regression summary for ring_edit coefficient (OR, CI, p-value)."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In drug-likeness tasks (task_id=103), edits that improve QED at the expense of synthetic accessibility (QED_delta>0 and SA_delta<0) are less likely to succeed compared to edits that improve both metrics or trade in the opposite direction.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare success rates among edit categories defined by sign of QED_delta and SA_delta for task 103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, filter to task_id==103; add success flag.\n2. Drop rows with null QED_delta or SA_delta.\n3. Define category: \n   A: QED_delta>0 & SA_delta<0\n   B: QED_delta>0 & SA_delta>=0\n   C: QED_delta<=0 & SA_delta<0\n   D: QED_delta<=0 & SA_delta>=0\n4. Tabulate success counts and rates in each category.\n5. Perform a chi-square test for equality of success rates across categories.\n6. Compute pairwise comparisons of success proportions with Bonferroni correction.\n7. Visualize success rates by category.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of counts and success rates by category.\n2. Chi-square test result and pairwise proportion test results.\n3. Bar chart of success rates across categories."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The impact of action_type on degree of target property change (target_delta) varies across optimization tasks (task_id), indicating a significant interaction.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for an interaction effect between task_id and action_type on target_delta among successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non-null target_delta.\n2. Encode task_id and action_type as categorical factors.\n3. Fit two-way ANOVA: target_delta ~ C(task_id) + C(action_type) + C(task_id):C(action_type).\n4. Examine significance of the interaction term.\n5. If significant, for selected tasks, plot interaction profiles (mean target_delta by action_type).\n6. Report effect sizes (η²) for main and interaction effects.",  
      "deliverables":"1. ANOVA table (F-statistics, p-values, η²).\n2. Interaction plots for illustrative task_ids.\n3. Interpretation of interaction."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Failure probability is higher for edits with extreme QED_delta values (lowest and highest 5% quantiles), controlling for task_id.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether extreme increases or decreases in QED correlate with higher failure odds.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets; add success flag; drop null QED_delta.\n2. Compute the 5th and 95th percentiles of QED_delta across the combined data.\n3. Create an extreme_flag: 1 if QED_delta ≤5th percentile or ≥95th percentile, else 0.\n4. Fit a logistic regression: success ~ extreme_flag + C(task_id).\n5. Extract coefficient, odds ratio, and p-value for extreme_flag.\n6. Plot predicted failure probability for extreme vs. non-extreme edits by task_id.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Percentile thresholds and counts in extreme_flag groups.\n2. Logistic regression summary for extreme_flag.\n3. Plot of predicted failure probability by extreme_flag and task."  
    }  
  }  
]}
